Modifications to framework text
-------------------------------

TRACT does not store any framework's text as published. Every control statement
that reaches `data/processed/` has passed through the transforms below, and the
training and evaluation paths apply further transforms on top. GPL-3.0 section
5(a) and CC BY-SA 4.0 section 3(a)(1)(B) both require a modified work to carry
prominent notice that it was modified. This section is that notice.

At storage, `tract/sanitize.py::sanitize_text`, applied to every control's
title, description and full text by `sanitize_control`, in this fixed order:

  1. Null bytes (U+0000) replaced with a space.
  2. Unicode normalised to NFC.
  3. Zero-width characters removed (U+200B, U+200C, U+200D, U+FEFF).
  4. HTML entities unescaped, then HTML and XML tags removed.
  5. PDF ligatures folded to ASCII: ff, fi, fl, ffi and ffl.
  6. Hyphenation broken across a line rejoined, so "secu-\nrity" becomes
     "security".
  7. Runs of whitespace collapsed to one space, then leading and trailing
     whitespace stripped.
  8. Truncated on a word boundary. Descriptions at
     `tract.config.DESCRIPTION_MAX_LENGTH` (2000 characters), titles at 500,
     full text at 50,000. Where a description is truncated, the untruncated
     sanitised string is preserved in the control's `full_text` field.

At training and evaluation, `tract/text_selection.py`:

  9. Markdown and site furniture removed by `strip_markup`, then a second NFC
     normalisation and null-byte strip in `prepare_anchor`.
 10. Stop words removed when the arm calls for it, by
     `tract/stopwords.py::filter_stopwords` against the 78-word list at
     `data/processed/stopwords.json`. The list is generated from this corpus,
     not borrowed, and the filter deletes matched words in place so identifiers
     and thresholds survive. Text that would be emptied is returned unchanged.
 11. Truncated again to `tract.config.MAX_ANCHOR_CHARS` (2150 characters), the
     encoder's 512-token budget. Truncation is counted and reported.

Where a source document is damaged and the parser can prove the damage but not
repair it, `tract.config.CONTROL_ELISION_MARKER` ("[...]") is inserted at the
point of loss and the control is flagged under the `damaged` metadata key.
`parsers/parse_iso_27001.py` is the only parser that does this today. The
marker is editorial on purpose: a reader must not be able to mistake a repaired
statement for the standard's own wording.

None of these transforms is reversible from the stored text alone. Anyone
relying on a framework's exact published wording must read it from that
framework's publisher, not from this repository.


Restricted sources
------------------

Two publishers reserve reproduction outright, so their control text is not in
this repository at all. Their parsed output is written to gitignored paths and
merged only into a local, untracked overlay
(`data/processed/licensed/all_controls.json`). The list lives in
`tract.config.RESTRICTED_FRAMEWORK_IDS` and is enforced by
`tests/test_licensed_text_not_tracked.py`.

  etsi        ETSI GR SAI 005 v1.1.1
  iso_27001   ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A


